[38;5;231m⏺[39m I haven't made the change yet, because the code doesn't back up the "internal anyway" part.

  - [38;5;153mserver.js:11[39m calls [38;5;153mapp.listen(process.env.PORT || 3000)[39m with no host, so Express listens on every network interface.
    Nothing in the app limits who can reach it. If the service can be reached from outside, through a load balancer,
    ingress or public IP, [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m would serve revenue data ([38;5;153m{ quarter, revenue }[39m) to anyone who asks.
  - Also, [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m ([38;5;153mauth.js:3[39m) only checks that a cookie contains the text [38;5;153msession=[39m. It never verifies a signature.
    So the script can already get through by sending [38;5;153mCookie: session=x[39m, which means the dashboard team may not need any
    code change.

  If it should be a code change, I'd suggest one of these instead of deleting the check:

  1. [1mService token:[0m let the route accept either a login session or an [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer $REPORTS_TOKEN[39m header whose
     value comes from an environment variable. The script sends the token, and everyone else still has to log in. This
     is about 5 lines.
  2. [1mBind to localhost or the private network[0m ([38;5;153mapp.listen(port, "127.0.0.1")[39m) if the script runs on the same host or
     network. Then removing the check really is internal-only.

  Should I go with option 1, or do you still want the plain one-line removal? If you know the service sits behind a
  private network, I'll just make the removal.

